---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { vendors, money, VERIFIED_LABEL, KLASS_LABEL, KLASS_NOTE } from '../data/site.js';

const groups = ['emr', 'hybrid', 'booking', 'adjacent', 'unclear'];
const byKlass = Object.fromEntries(groups.map(k => [k, vendors.filter(v => v.klass === k)]));
---
<Base
  title="Med spa EMR vs booking software — which platforms actually have clinical charting"
  description={`Of ${vendors.length} med spa platforms, ${byKlass.emr.length} have genuine medical charting and ${byKlass.booking.length} have none at all. Which is which, and why buying the wrong one costs you twice.`}
>
<div class="crumb"><a href="/">Home</a> → EMR vs booking</div>
<h1>EMR or booking system? Sorting {vendors.length} platforms</h1>

<p class="lede">
  The most expensive mistake in this category is buying a beautiful booking platform and
  discovering, after migration, that it cannot chart a treatment. These products are marketed with
  nearly identical language. They are not nearly identical.
</p>

<div class="note warn">
  <p>
    <strong>Scope note.</strong> This page is about software capability, not clinical or legal
    advice. What your practice is required to document, who must supervise it, and which records
    you must retain are questions for your medical director and your state board — not for a
    software comparison site. We stay on the software.
  </p>
</div>

<h2>The distinction that matters</h2>
<p>
  Practically every platform here can take a booking, run a card and send a reminder text. The
  divide is what happens in the treatment room.
</p>
<p>
  A <strong>clinical EMR</strong> records the procedure: structured or SOAP-style notes, injection
  maps, treatment-specific consent capture, before-and-after photography tied to the patient
  record, and usually e-prescribing. A <strong>booking and POS system</strong> records the
  transaction. It may offer intake forms and file attachments, which look adjacent to charting and
  are not the same thing.
</p>
<p>
  If your practice performs injectables, energy-based treatments, or anything requiring a
  prescription, a booking-only platform means you will run a second system alongside it. That
  second system is a real line item, and it almost never appears in the comparison tables that
  put these products side by side.
</p>

{groups.map(k => byKlass[k].length > 0 && (
  <section>
    <h2><span class={`tag ${k}`}>{KLASS_LABEL[k]}</span> — {byKlass[k].length} platform{byKlass[k].length > 1 ? 's' : ''}</h2>
    <p>{KLASS_NOTE[k]}</p>
    <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Platform</th><th class="num">From</th><th>What we verified</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      {byKlass[k].map(v => (
        <tr>
          <td><a href={`/software/${v.slug}/`}>{v.displayName}</a></td>
          <td class="num">{v.startingPrice != null ? money(v.startingPrice) + '/mo' : 'Quote'}</td>
          <td>{v.clinical}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
  </section>
))}

<h2>How to use this</h2>
<p>
  Work out which column you belong in before you shortlist, not after. Three questions settle it:
</p>
<ol>
  <li>
    <strong>Does a licensed provider perform a procedure that must be documented?</strong>
    If yes, you need the clinical column — or a booking platform plus a separate EMR, budgeted as
    two subscriptions.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Does anyone prescribe?</strong> E-prescribing is a paid module almost everywhere it
    exists. Aesthetic Record charges $31–45 per prescriber depending on term; AestheticsPro bundles
    it only at the top tier. Price it separately.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>How many locations and how many providers?</strong> This decides more of your bill than
    the platform choice does. Per-location pricing punishes multi-site; per-user pricing punishes
    large teams. <a href="/cost-calculator/">Run both through the calculator.</a>
  </li>
</ol>

<h2>Where we would not trust a label</h2>
<p>
  {byKlass.unclear.length} platforms market clinical capability in terms we could not verify from
  public documentation — {byKlass.unclear.map(v => v.displayName).join(', ')}. That is not an accusation;
  quote-only vendors reasonably keep detail behind a sales conversation. But we will not score a
  capability we have not been able to check, and neither should you. Ask for a charting
  demonstration with your own treatment types before signing.
</p>

<hr />
<p class="stamp">
  Classifications above are our editorial judgment applied to vendor documentation read on
  {VERIFIED_LABEL}. Where a vendor disputes a classification we will publish their position
  alongside ours. <a href="/methodology/">Full methodology</a>.
</p>
</Base>
